event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_04bcf025ef129da7006a5f8b2999bc8195ae8ff83c2eb32f44","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":276,"text":"The latest notable Perseverance rover news is that NASA’s rover has **completed a “marathon” on Mars**: it has driven **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** across the Red Planet. It reached that distance on its **1,890th Martian day**, about **five years and four months** after landing—much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance.【5:0†source】\n\nNASA’s recent Perseverance updates also highlight new science from the rover’s exploration beyond Jezero Crater. The rover has been studying a layered rock unit called the **“Broom Point member”** on Jezero’s rim, likely more than **3.9 billion years old**. NASA says Perseverance found evidence that a roughly **245-foot / 75-meter-thick** stack of ancient rock was built by **repeated asteroid impacts**, giving scientists a rare look at very early Martian geologic history.【5:1†source】\n\nNASA’s Perseverance news page lists several mid-July 2026 updates, including a **sweeping view of Broom Point**, a map of the rover’s trip there, and the article on ancient Mars impacts—showing the rover is actively working in terrain outside Jezero Crater’s western rim.【5:2†source】"}

